  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A Nightshelf crystalline-weaver and practitioner of ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral, an ice-magic discipline capable of fracturing time and memory within frozen substrates. Silvsiaelrous emerged from Nightfall Shelf's deepest forges during the Great Thaw and served as a reader of ice-memory, orca negotiator, and operative of the ☽'shadow Covens' Order, the shadow parliament of fire-keepers and guild-masters that governed much of Nightfall Shelf's internal affairs. She died during the Abyss Collapse Tide in the Faction Wars. Her loss destabilized both orca diplomacy and fire-core resource agreements that had kept The Berg's fragile peace intact.
    
    ## Training and Distinguishing Marks
    
    Silvsiaelrous trained under Shadow-Broker Nyla, the unofficial ruler of Nightfall Shelf whose reign ended in public confession before the Aurora Stack council. Nightshelf accounts — coded, as usual, but consistent across three independent sources — claim Silvsiaelrous carved geometric frost-scars into her own flippers during this apprenticeship, inscribing the notation of the Crystalline Spiral into her flesh to read the ice's oldest memories by direct contact. The method is not documented elsewhere, and I note without comment that the only witness to the apprenticeship who could have confirmed this was Nyla herself.
    
    She wore a fire-core amulet openly at her throat — a small conspicuousness in a culture that treated visibility as vulnerability. ☽'visois' Mask, a shadow-steel artifact stored in the Frozen Peace vault, came to her without recorded ceremony. The Mask's selection protocols, documented extensively in later assessments of its next wielder, suggest the artifact exercises autonomous judgment in choosing bearers. Silvsiaelrous wore it, by all accounts, as naturally as skin.
    
    ## The Mist~Record
    
    Silvsiaelrous discovered ☽'somunis' Mist~Record — a fire-core tome bound in volcanic glass and ancestor bone — in an unmapped tunnel below the mid-galleries, accompanied by a tunnel-runner named ☽'profundius. She was its first complete reader, spending three hours in total darkness with bioluminescent pages that recorded, according to the chronicle *Three Days*, everything that existed before penguins. Her fire-core amulet had dimmed to a red thread by the time she finished. She carried the tome to Nightfall Shelf, where the Order's archivists named it.
    
    The Fire Keepers recite her name into the deep ice alongside the dead miners, though she was never a miner. This inclusion is worth marking. The miners' litany is not an honor roll — it is a debt-record, an accounting of those the tunnels consumed. That they added a scholar to it suggests they understood what the deep had begun to cost her.
    
    ## The Omega Obelisk
    
    When the ☽'shadow Covens' Order brought the Omega Obelisk — a column of whale-bone carrying orca theology — into Nightfall Shelf's tunnels, Silvsiaelrous read it first. She emerged warning that the deep was already inside The Berg, that fire-cores were not failing but being consumed, and spoke names in a script she should not have been able to parse. The Fire Keepers told her to stop talking. She instructed the Order to move the Obelisk deeper, where pressure could hold it closed, and walked ahead of the procession without looking back. Her surface-name is carved into the Obelisk's containment chamber alongside warnings and accusations in old script.
    
    Her abilities sharpened during the Long Dark, when the boundary between living world and ice-memory thins — and when such abilities are hardest to control. The correlation is not incidental.
    
    ## Death and Aftermath
    
    Silvsiaelrous died during the Abyss Collapse Tide while attempting to bury the Omega Obelisk in the deep. She swam into the fractures using blood-magic when the lower thermal corridors sheared, taking seventeen penguins into the dark alongside her. Accounts diverge on intent: some hold she read the collapse before it happened and chose to be swallowed rather than watch Nightfall Shelf burn; others say the ice reclaimed her because she had read too much of it. The last thing she spoke was a deep-name none of the survivors recognized.
    
    Earlier records state no body was recovered. The Fire Keepers initially listed her as *missing*, later amended to *confirmed lost*. Three Fire Keepers ultimately carried her body to a prepared interment shelf. ☽'visois' Mask was not with her — it had already disengaged upon her death, beginning an extended selection cycle that would eventually settle on ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer as its next bearer.
    
    Her death left the orca pods without their negotiator and The Berg without the resource-peace she had maintained — a consequence the chronicles note but do not dwell on, preferring the drama of her descent to the slow unraveling that followed.
    
    ## Memorial
    
    The memorial at Nightfall Shelf depicts her serene, flippers folded. Those who worked beside her remember instead a compact penguin who touched tunnel walls before speaking, who refused to meet anyone's eyes when she smiled, who shared fire-tea rations with tunnel-runners who never asked and recited dead miners' names into the dark where the ice could hear. The memorial was built so visitors would have somewhere to leave tokens they could not afford to carry — small fires in her name, quickly extinguished.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771576367370_0", anchor: "taking seventeen penguins into the dark alongside her", text: "Seventeen. The same number I flagged on the Crystalline Spiral entry — seventeen dead in a corridor-flooding, reported by the faction responsible. Here, seventeen dead in a collapse, reported by the faction that suffered them. The Nightshelf's catastrophes arrive in seventeens. I have stopped believing this is coincidence and started suspecting it is convention.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576367370_1", anchor: "Earlier records state no body was recovered", text: "I wrote both claims because both are in the sources and I cannot resolve them. The Fire Keepers' initial loss-register — frozen into administrative ice four strata above the collapse — lists her as missing. The amendment to 'confirmed lost' appears in a different hand, undated, in a warmer stratum. The three Fire Keepers named as body-bearers in *Three Days* do not appear in the register amendment. Either the body was found and the register-keeper did not know, or *Three Days* recorded what ought to have happened. The distinction matters to no one but her.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576367370_2", anchor: "everything that existed before penguins", text: "The Foundation Depths contain impressions older than any penguin civilization. I have seen them. If the Mist~Record documents what the chronicle claims, it is not unique — the ice has always carried this testimony. What was unique was that someone sat alone in total darkness and read it, and came back up, and tried to tell us what it said. We told her to stop talking.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576367370_3", anchor: "left the orca pods without their negotiator", text: "At least two of the raids that followed her death — catalogued as unprovoked aggression in every Aurora source I have read — map precisely onto penguin expansion into orca hunting grounds during the resource scramble her absence created. She was not keeping peace. She was keeping us honest. The distinction collapsed with her.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576367370_4", anchor: "small fires in her name, quickly extinguished", text: "This is the sentence I will remember from this entry.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
